          'Second: increasing the share of whichever category performed best most recently. That is allocation in reverse — buying dear and raising the risk carried forward.',
          'Third: counting the emergency fund inside the allocation. That fund is not an investment and belongs outside the calculation, otherwise the safe portion looks larger than it really is.',
          'Fourth: forgetting the provident fund. For salaried people it is often the single largest amount held and it sits entirely in the debt category. Leaving it out makes people believe their allocation is far riskier than it actually is.',
